<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 70 0 R
/T (- Logistic regression instead of a more complex model: the feature set is small and the relationship between features and success is expected to be roughly monotonic \(more mastery, more recent success, less difficulty should raise the probability\), so a linear-in-log-odds model is both sufficient and easy to explain through its coefficients.)
/E (- Logistic regression instead of a more complex model: the feature set is small and the relationship between features and success is expected to be roughly monotonic \(more mastery, more recent success, less difficulty should raise the probability\), so a linear-in-log-odds model is both sufficient and easy to explain through its coefficients.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 70 0 R
/T (- Beam search instead of a full exhaustive search or a greedy pick: a greedy pick can get stuck always picking the single highest utility topic every slot, since the repetition penalty is what prevents that, but a greedy method reacts to the penalty one step late. A full exhaustive search over action sequences grows quickly with the number of topics and slots. Beam search keeps a small set of the most promising partial plans at each step, which is a good middle ground for a CLI tool that has to feel instant.)
/E (- Beam search instead of a full exhaustive search or a greedy pick: a greedy pick can get stuck always picking the single highest utility topic every slot, since the repetition penalty is what prevents that, but a greedy method reacts to the penalty one step late. A full exhaustive search over action sequences grows quickly with the number of topics and slots. Beam search keeps a small set of the most promising partial plans at each step, which is a good middle ground for a CLI tool that has to feel instant.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
