211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (A strong pass \(say 0.95\) adds more to alpha than a narrow pass \(0.62\), and a strong fail adds more to beta than a narrow fail. Mastery is read as alpha / \(alpha + beta\); uncertainty is the standard deviation of the Beta\(alpha, beta\) distribution, sqrt\(alpha*beta / \(\(alpha+beta\)^2 * \(alpha+beta+1\)\)\). As attempts accumulate, alpha + beta grows, and the standard deviation shrinks, which is exactly the 'more evidence, less uncertainty' behaviour Bayesian updating is meant to give. This is verified directly in test_mastery.py \(test_uncertainty_decreases_with_more_attempts\).)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (A lightweight forgetting mechanism \(apply_forgetting\) pulls alpha and beta slightly back toward 1 the longer a topic has gone unstudied, on an exponential half life curve, so mastery estimates for topics the student has not touched in weeks drift back toward uncertainty rather than staying frozen at their last known value.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
