/P 71 0 R
/E (The success predictor is a scikit-learn LogisticRegression trained on features standardized with StandardScaler. The seven input features are: current mastery, recent average score \(last three results\), latest score, topic difficulty, days since last study, attempts, and exam urgency \(derived from days left before the subject's exam date\). The target label is a synthetic binary 'success' outcome.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (Logistic regression was chosen over alternatives such as random forests or gradient boosting because the project asks for an explainable model, and because the training set is small \(a few hundred synthetic rows\). A linear model's coefficients can be read directly as the direction and rough size of each feature's effect on the log odds of success, which is printed to the user through the 'Show ML model info' menu option. A more flexible model would risk overfitting a dataset this size and would not add any explanatory value the user could act on.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 71 0 R
/E (Dataset description: the synthetic dataset is generated by src/ml_model.py \(generate_synthetic_dataset\) with a fixed random seed. It draws feature values from simple distributions \(mastery and exam urgency uniform on 0 to 1, days since study and attempts from bounded integer ranges\) and derives a success probability from a weighted combination of the features passed through a logistic function, then samples the binary label from that probability. Sample size is 400 rows. This is a synthetic dataset built to give the classifier a plausible, documented relationship to learn; it is not drawn from or claimed to represent any real student population.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
