225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 72 0 R
/T (The planner is a beam search over sequences of 20 minute study actions. The state is the remaining time budget, how many times each topic has already been used in this plan, and the actions chosen so far. At each step, every candidate \(subject, topic\) pair still under its repeat limit is scored with the utility function below, a new action is created, and the resulting state is added to the pool of candidates for the next step. The pool is sorted by total accumulated utility and trimmed to the beam width \(default 4\) before the next step. This bounds the search to beam_width x candidates work per step instead of growing combinatorially with the number of slots.)
/E (The planner is a beam search over sequences of 20 minute study actions. The state is the remaining time budget, how many times each topic has already been used in this plan, and the actions chosen so far. At each step, every candidate \(subject, topic\) pair still under its repeat limit is scored with the utility function below, a new action is created, and the resulting state is added to the pool of candidates for the next step. The pool is sorted by total accumulated utility and trimmed to the beam width \(default 4\) before the next step. This bounds the search to beam_width x candidates work per step instead of growing combinatorially with the number of slots.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 72 0 R
/T (Recent average score and current mastery have the largest positive weight, which matches the intuitive expectation that a student who has been doing well recently and already knows the topic reasonably well is more likely to have a successful next session. Days since last study has a negative weight, consistent with rustier topics being harder to succeed at immediately. The negative weight on exam_urgency reflects a pattern in the synthetic generation formula and should be read as a property of the training data, not a real claim that urgency itself makes a session harder; this is flagged here as a limitation of training on a synthetic formula rather than real behavioural data.)
/E (Recent average score and current mastery have the largest positive weight, which matches the intuitive expectation that a student who has been doing well recently and already knows the topic reasonably well is more likely to have a successful next session. Days since last study has a negative weight, consistent with rustier topics being harder to succeed at immediately. The negative weight on exam_urgency reflects a pattern in the synthetic generation formula and should be read as a property of the training data, not a real claim that urgency itself makes a session harder; this is flagged here as a limitation of training on a synthetic formula rather than real behavioural data.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 72 0 R
/T (Bias and variance considerations: with only seven features and a linear model, the predictor sits on the higher-bias, lower-variance side, which is a reasonable choice for a small dataset since a higher variance model \(for example an unpruned decision tree\) would likely fit noise in only 300 training rows. The roughly 75 percent test accuracy is treated in the application as a moderately confident signal to blend into the planner's utility function, not as a precise probability.)
/E (Bias and variance considerations: with only seven features and a linear model, the predictor sits on the higher-bias, lower-variance side, which is a reasonable choice for a small dataset since a higher variance model \(for example an unpruned decision tree\) would likely fit noise in only 300 training rows. The roughly 75 percent test accuracy is treated in the application as a moderately confident signal to blend into the planner's utility function, not as a precise probability.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
